WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.449
Film Courage: [I’m] wondering with the $25,000
initial grant [for THE MILLIONAIRES’ UNIT],

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:07.910
when did you realize you needed more and then
how did you end up getting more funding?

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:08.910
What was your process?

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.030
Darroch Greer, Co-Filmmaker of The Millionaires’
Unit: Well, we knew we needed more and we

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:18.890
got to know two grandsons of the First Yale
Unit Members (Harry Davison in Manhattan and

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:25.349
Mike Davison in Atlanta) they are cousins
and share a grandfather Trubee Davison and

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:28.359
we asked them to be on our fundraising committee.

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:32.290
I think for three and a half years every Thursday
morning at 6:00 a.m.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:37.980
(West Coast time) we would talk to them about
people they knew, foundations they might be

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.880
aware of to raise money and we wrote a lot
of letters.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.690
And Ron and I we’d go these coasts to interview
people and do production and film planes or

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:48.690
whatever we’d do.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.480
Then we’d go to New York and show whatever
we had at the Dolby Screening Room there and

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.650
try to get people to write checks for us there.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:01.990
And we’d get $100 here and a $100 there
and it wasn’t really doing it.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:08.530
But Ron started writing a quarterly newsletter
and we started collecting email addresses

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:13.510
and sending this out and letting them know
it was going and gradually it got out to more

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.600
and more foundations.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.200
We learned that there was a level of foundations
and money that you don’t really hear about

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:31.159
that wealthy families have to give away X
amount of money every year for tax purposes.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:35.460
So there were people who had some relation
to the story whether it was through Yale,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.010
whether it was through the Navy or whether
they just wanted to see a patriotic story

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.540
being told.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.820
And we kept trying for a corporate sponsor.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:54.790
We worked on JPMorgan for over two years because
originally Trubee Davison, the young guy who

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.659
had founded the unit’s father, was a banking
business partner.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:04.570
And the bank had given the first Yale Unit
$100,000 to get started and buy airplanes

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:10.090
and so we knew some people working there and
said “We’d like the same amount of money,

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.630
adjusted for inflation and we were asking
for $200,000.”

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:20.690
All the time we were working on it, every
week in the news there would just be horrible

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:28.140
reports about corporate behavior and its CEO
and they’ve done this and they’ve done

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.140
that.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:35.000
We still wanted the money and in the end it
fell through and I think sort of out of embarrassment

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.390
and kindness they gave us $25,000, which helped.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:45.280
In the end our corporate sponsor was FedEx
and we had wanted to go to them for a long

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:51.209
time because Fred Smith (the founder) went
to Yale, he started the the Yale Aero Club

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:52.569
and I think he was a Marine?

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:58.880
Or he flew with the Navy (I think in the early
70’s) or at least during the early 70’s.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:04.630
And so we were sure he’d be at all aware
of the story and indeed it was a story that

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:05.989
really meant something to him.

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:12.210
He was also a member of Skull and Bones the
way probably 5 of these First Unit Yale guys

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.270
were, as well.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:20.560
And so in the end, yeah they came through
with the last $150,000 that we need to help

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.560
finish the film.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.280
Ron King, Co-Filmmaker of The Millionaires’
Unit: In terms of the other little bit on

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:30.730
fundraising that I’ll mention is that our
fundraising was always driven by necessity.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:37.630
It wasn’t like we were trying to raise money
randomly to say “Oh we would like to raise

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.580
$250,000.”

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.130
Nothing was it like that.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.590
It was always based on necessity.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:50.140
So as we were developing the script and we
started editing, we’d realize there was

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:57.959
a big chunk that we needed to do somewhere
to film or a group of people we need to interview

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:02.680
and then we’d budget for those particular
parts of the film.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:08.590
So yes we had an overall budget for the film,
but as we went along over those 7 years, we

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:13.959
kind of did mini-budgets where we said “Oh
we need to do that particular shoot.

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:16.090
How much is that particular shoot going to
cost?

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.090
It’s going to cost…”

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:22.879
And we’d break it all down, travel, whatever
else was involved.

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:29.460
And then our fundraising efforts would drive
toward fulfilling that particular chunk.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:35.979
Darroch Greer: So in the newsletter we’d
say, we need $25,000 to film a Sopwith Camel

00:04:35.979 --> 00:04:42.569
or we need $20,000 for our composer, those
were two things that we really put out there

00:04:42.569 --> 00:04:45.210
saying “This is the next big thing we need.”

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:49.289
And we would be out of money at each of these
stages.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:52.680
Film Courage: And this was a quarterly newsletter
you said that you made?

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:59.050
Ron King: It was intended to be a quarterly
newsletter and most of the time it was quarterly,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.110
a couple times it was off-schedule but generally
it was quarterly.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:07.740
Film Courage: I find that fascinating, so
you would weave in different things or stories

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.449
[into the newsletter] and then you would have
what was next for the final part of the film?

00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:19.779
Ron King: As a matter of fact, the first thing
we did before we even started fundraising

00:05:19.779 --> 00:05:25.370
seriously was that we actually put our website
together first.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:29.339
Which was kind of a little weird way of doing
it.

00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:35.110
And what we did as part of our website, we
put up the imagery that we already had and

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:42.740
we started to create kind of a historical,
online museum as it were and the website that

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:45.909
we put up is actually still up there today.

00:05:45.909 --> 00:05:54.710
But that gave us kind of somewhere where our
potential contributors, followers could go

00:05:54.710 --> 00:06:01.069
and see what we were about and what we were
trying to do over time and as people got on

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:07.590
the email list, there was the added reach
(outreach) with the newsletter.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:13.819
Darroch Greer: Actually the very first thing
we did after we put together the non-profit

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:18.550
organization and got our board together (which
were friends of ours) is Ron and I put together

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:22.639
a PowerPoint presentation of what the story
was and they all fell asleep.

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:25.080
Film Courage: Oh no!

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.210
Was this at some kind of a mixer or something?

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:31.830
Darroch Greer: No it was at my house and here’s
the story and I applied them with wine…

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:32.830
Film Courage: That was the problem.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.509
Darroch Greer: And they all fell asleep.

